## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of privacy-preserving distributed learning and the real-world trade-offs of deploying it at scale, including regulatory considerations and operational costs.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning
- Differential privacy
- Secure aggregation
- Model drift and data poisoning
- Rollout strategy and cost modeling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: simulate a DP-guarded update
function dpUpdate(weights, clip, sigma){
  // clip
  let clipped = weights.map(w => Math.max(-clip, Math.min(clip, w)));
  // add noise
  return clipped.map(w => w + randn(0, sigma));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure break-even and what data would you collect during rollout?
- What privacy/regulatory trade-offs would influence DP epsilon targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to model hybrid edge-cloud workloads, latency, and regulatory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid inference and offload decisions
- Resource budgets: RAM, energy, bandwidth
- Privacy/regulatory overhead and risk scoring
- Rollout strategy, drift, maintenance, and KPIs

## Code Example
```javascript
function estimateCBA(params){
  // naive cost model: local vs cloud with token costs and energy
  const localEnergy = params.localInferenceMs * params.energyPerMs;
  const cloudCost = params.tokens * params.tokenPrice;
  return {monthly: localEnergy + cloudCost, breakEvenMonths: 24};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate accuracy relative to cost? What metrics drive threshold changes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design privacy-preserving, offline-first collaborative systems at scale. It tests technical understanding of cryptographic protocols, peer-to-peer networking, CRDT algorithms, and cost-benefit analysis while considering regulatory compliance and user experience constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Privacy-preserving collaborative editing
- CRDT convergence and conflict-free resolution
- Peer-to-peer mesh topology and infrastructure costs
- End-to-end encryption with lifecycle key management
- Compliance logging and audit trail requirements
- Performance trade-offs between latency and device resource consumption

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple OR-Set CRDT merge (illustrative)
function mergeORSet(A, B) {
  const merged = new Set([...A.added, ...B.added]);
  [...A.removed, ...B.removed].forEach(item => merged.delete(item));
  return merged;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a cost-aware, privacy-preserving XAI feature with on-device constraints and compliance.

## Key Concepts

- On-device explainability budgets, latency targets, and data locality
- Telemetry storage limits, retention, and privacy controls
- Cost trade-offs between local inference vs cloud-audit

## Code Example

```javascript
function capLogs(logs, maxBytes) {
  // Simplified capping by character length
  let acc = '';
  for (const s of logs) {
    if ((acc + s).length <= maxBytes) acc += s;
    else break;
  }
  return acc;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle model drift and auditability across OS updates?
- What metrics would you track for go/no-go decisions and how would you set thresholds?

